ue "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2afc783b-b9ed-5bd3-afa0-161d31d4cab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250919} :answer-field/id #uuid "174407a9-f46d-55da-8697-5dbc5ce40bfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6494dcea-ff4c-57dc-b8ab-235d70e87dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-119361" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee277f-7d39-51d6-ae8e-804455cc5483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328112} {:answer/id #uuid "9908e0fd-b391-508e-8dd9-9f1b7572eaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328256} {:answer/id #uuid "b457a22e-59a5-554f-b4d6-d662134d1479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328503} {:answer/id #uuid "d0528873-0750-5fae-828d-e2dd5c23f87e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f4dfaf9-6adc-58e2-af62-11084821e994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358} :answer-field/id #uuid "a085cb5f-e013-5736-9ee5-2fef11903a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329453}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770be8a8-209f-5a4a-8f5b-267370ed95fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-43288" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efce697-3641-5b14-b027-ecd2b195e7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327067} {:answer/id #uuid "24edcb9d-3e64-5b73-8fd1-94c24bf76145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327254} {:answer/id #uuid "567f1bcb-a850-52d9-9c67-5c37ad657066" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327683} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9f0214-9783-5eb9-916e-a5dd1fa9da94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327774} {:answer/id #uuid "a00a08ef-de95-5fd8-a811-22199591e5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00a08ef-de95-5fd8-a811-22199591e5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328316} :answer-field/id #uuid "26560cc9-2457-555a-b1fc-f39375ebfef3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe9bc87-3892-5729-86c2-f5e953c42646" :question/math-academy-id "q-43293" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }({sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d18f305-7184-570c-9038-db639fb9c690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333104} {:answer/id #uuid "1ef194b7-a3bd-5a62-88d6-f95203c27b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333262} {:answer/id #uuid "35f8ee1e-2532-55f4-ad12-ee7fd5baa04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333487} {:answer/id #uuid "76b09143-8785-5fd3-a8de-148adecf998d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334059} {:answer/id #uuid "b2496fe5-9af3-5558-80cb-2b41bd7291a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d18f305-7184-570c-9038-db639fb9c690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333104} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8dd284f-c749-5630-9eb7-44cca829df55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335646}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33f7f09b-8fbf-5a12-9433-87f274f8c827" :question/math-academy-id "q-65222" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡t(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{sin}^{2}⁡t=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29867d59-2029-5173-b108-462543ae8b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333375} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0ea8ff-9e84-5428-97fe-3086a3798ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333427} {:answer/id #uuid "4661ca1a-94ef-5f31-a526-91738f843cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333641} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3f92c5-ad36-5075-a9ce-39be43e43e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333851} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ee91a-2fdf-5e88-a4b9-c50b26f48481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e3f92c5-ad36-5075-a9ce-39be43e43e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333851} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e35aa7-20df-59e9-bd4c-4cb8baf51d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335312}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e0bfa0a-1f0f-581c-9a6b-34627aa91d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-65225" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t & ={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t=1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Expanding Parentheses and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059434 :question/id #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" :question/math-academy-id "e-13411" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\n- $(a-2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\n- $(a+2, b-6)$\n\n- $(a-2,b+ 6)$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2{)}^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf13dd1-d493-55fb-94ad-c50574dc8bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253778} {:answer/id #uuid "71186c58-32dc-5b95-a447-4b4569b59c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254672} {:answer/id #uuid "a275b10b-0be9-59e6-8c33-ce2b9dcd6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255104} {:answer/id #uuid "b66ae15a-a2f1-5d79-b5f4-12bbbae7e98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255286} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b76ad7-f3fe-559a-8617-6ec58d9ce1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a275b10b-0be9-59e6-8c33-ce2b9dcd6958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+1,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255104} :answer-field/id #uuid "11178a13-a5c1-5fd6-b0f8-608b3946af04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255943}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "933f2dda-2343-544a-8ef3-2160dd14e9e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-121220" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{2}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215b733e-02f5-559d-b497-8039bd0abddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290773} {:answer/id #uuid "40347c00-b783-5421-939f-ab3ef7825eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290900} {:answer/id #uuid "5451ee39-3132-55be-b496-cccbb1ecfcd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290991} {:answer/id #uuid "669f54dd-330e-5187-a457-8ec777fa2db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291068} {:answer/id #uuid "832359a3-815f-50ef-8abf-011a2185b994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "669f54dd-330e-5187-a457-8ec777fa2db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291068} :answer-field/id #uuid "7865af2d-939b-59a2-9e75-cdf7738a3599" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292113}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4d7f8b8-ba0a-5db0-954f-1969ddfefa81" :question/math-academy-id "q-26398" :question/problem "The point $(1,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x-1)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233288 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00e41b54-1b06-55e4-ba21-6bbf8797df49" :question/math-academy-id "q-121381" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b-1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$, we have that $\\sqrt{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{a+1-1}-1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{a}-1 \\\\  & =b-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b-1)$ must lie on $y=\\sqrt{x-1}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233289 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d1f17ba-35cc-58b5-9aa2-08d577267c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121393" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={x}^{3}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=2(x+2)^{3},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{3}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of  $2$ parallel to the $y$ -axis and get the graph of  $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-2,b)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(a-2,2b).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{3}$, we have that ${a}^{3}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$ Substituting $x=a-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(a-2+2)^{3} \\\\  & =2{a}^{3} \\\\  & =2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-2,2b)$ must lie on $y=2(x+2)^{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233534 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a80933c-10f7-51d8-9b1a-039e3550e2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158540" :question/problem "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e3ec5-5bc9-5cba-9c8a-6b6ebe75a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253863} {:answer/id #uuid "5828d10b-52d2-519e-94d5-63d2d5b4c550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254445} {:answer/id #uuid "74fc90a5-c321-5fe2-a988-759338bbb191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,3b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254707} {:answer/id #uuid "b59d2443-c7bf-54dc-9108-20e2c63d903b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255271} {:answer/id #uuid "c709185f-e827-59eb-bd52-699c51a7e1b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b59d2443-c7bf-54dc-9108-20e2c63d903b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255271} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca534256-c181-5ac0-b56d-2d5a591a2172" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b584cb7-ff23-5cf8-a53c-0371ad6cd2e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121383" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c21c55-17bb-5c41-88d1-6c299c881b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-2,b-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254253} {:answer/id #uuid "80df87fd-c227-5716-9dc2-6b062371184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254808} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f3a22f-dbdf-598f-a34c-1777eda4f583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255276} {:answer/id #uuid "bbff3184-f777-51ba-8744-de29245ae71f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255349} {:answer/id #uuid "f87700c2-b05d-5438-9cf7-184870d44569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80df87fd-c227-5716-9dc2-6b062371184a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a-3,b-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254808} :answer-field/id #uuid "074aa316-c74e-53ab-a8c8-8f76eb00e9c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255928}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19d2f443-e38d-5ded-a277-4288cfd36f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-121385" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=|x|.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=|x+3|-2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=|x+3|-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=|x|.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=|x+3|.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a-3,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a-3,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=|x|$, we have that $|a|=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=|x+3|-2.$ Substituting $x=a-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =|a-3+3|-2 \\\\  & =|a|-2 \\\\  & =b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a-3,b-2)$ must lie on $y=|x+3|-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e5bc30c-2abb-52a7-bb51-cb184ad226c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254343} {:answer/id #uuid "6cad43fd-a241-5e56-82fc-2eeac4a6bb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a+2,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254635} {:answer/id #uuid "81df30f7-1414-57bb-a6ef-282731df4980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({a}^{3/2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254821} {:answer/id #uuid "adde4716-7e5d-5b0d-b0ca-454d703c35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255204} {:answer/id #uuid "f6019e36-937d-5523-941c-72dc4b368337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{a}{2},b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adde4716-7e5d-5b0d-b0ca-454d703c35b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2a,b+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255204} :answer-field/id #uuid "4647e489-d352-5af5-8c97-6318e3c26f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d40ea2d8-4f8c-52c6-b88c-21d92eb5a3d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121399" :question/problem "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={3}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={3}^{x/2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={3}^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit up and obtain the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b+1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={3}^{x}$, we have that ${3}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={3}^{(2a)/2}+1 \\\\  & ={3}^{a}+1 \\\\  & =b+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b+1)$ must lie on $y={3}^{x/2}+1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} {:answer/id #uuid "487a2755-1c9b-50dc-a9ef-825d319c08d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290930} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5b7f1c-6ca6-519d-9324-afd97504caa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291021} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbeb462-5ad4-5751-9859-039c2c20e85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291457} {:answer/id #uuid "f4debbbb-a978-5aa2-807b-d09d5e6771d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} :answer-field/id #uuid "337977c2-17f5-5416-91d9-a9bbf15f1e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291907}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eb852b1-3670-5b7e-89d9-1b29c405f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26594" :question/problem "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059435 :question/id #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13412" :question/problem "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123d4004-5c38-5292-afe0-3926ee0d0bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056395} {:answer/id #uuid "215d0348-3325-5b0f-b430-be17a1c52b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056396} {:answer/id #uuid "37548807-d256-5fc9-98ef-bbc3e9fc2a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056397} {:answer/id #uuid "5376b742-5988-5f01-98d6-a68f5ec75b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056398} {:answer/id #uuid "5da47774-b4c3-5656-a5f3-c6639c1c26fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056399}] :answer-field/id #uuid "437656e2-e6a9-5e24-ad04-71e3bec79dec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056394}] :question/id #uuid "35f6ccdb-baf2-5629-ace1-fbbc52cdd1b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-121405" :question/problem "The point $(-2, 4)$ lies on the curve $y = 2f(x)$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y =-f(x) + 2$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bad3011-eec4-5bd6-9f12-9de90977d847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058177} {:answer/id #uuid "dba39df7-83e7-5fab-846e-119c803d8ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058178} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b7ee60-2fd8-523e-8b42-4824fc438dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058179} {:answer/id #uuid "272b5946-719d-5b75-850b-893b6e80fde6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058180} {:answer/id #uuid "6edc1855-3018-5684-a7e0-1d2d3b236b62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058181}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9c2bb2-bb19-5c69-a07c-605a418546e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058176}] :question/id #uuid "49ac0e7b-6d64-5ad3-b75e-0edbe3724d3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26595" :question/problem "The point $(-1, 1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) + 1$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y =-f(x) - 1$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233290 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66ee65e4-7115-5fe1-b74b-2344562d97c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-121431" :question/problem "The point  $(0,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+2).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0+2,1)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(1))=(2,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-1+2)=(2,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0+2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(2)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+2 \\\\  & =-(1)+2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233535 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6d8898-251c-5832-a715-c1e0b2d67196" :question/math-academy-id "q-158542" :question/problem "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233536 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcbd4637-6a8f-52c9-a823-7c7a1c3283b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158546" :question/problem "The point  $(2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-2).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3-f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=3-f(x):$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-2).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2-2,1)=(0,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=3-f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(1))=(0,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-1+3)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-2)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2-2)=1\\,⟹\\,f(0)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3-f(x).$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3-f(0) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,2)$ must lie on $y=3-f(x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233700 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d89896ee-0650-5ce4-90d0-9b6eb4318030" :question/math-academy-id "q-26597" :question/problem "The point  $(0,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0,4-2)=(0,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(0,-(2))=(0,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $3$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ After this transformation, the point $(0,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(0,-2-3)=(0,-5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0)+2=4\\,⟹\\,f(0)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-3.$ Substituting $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(0)-3 \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,-5)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a70a80-9905-5343-a34c-136a375e25ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251108} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ce837a-8964-565e-9e2d-f825bac56dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252014} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b4c14b-cbd8-5494-b7e3-3692ec97eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252455} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa8370-7c51-5755-8595-d094b48e3a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2019c876-575f-5be0-a410-079acd66193f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d38949b-9d50-5e0f-bb63-ea8ff36967c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-119308" :question/problem "The point $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34d52287-c3ec-5c1d-bc8c-f42f5d1b1173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254122} {:answer/id #uuid "48193e72-9f23-5387-b843-0b954ae77279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254280} {:answer/id #uuid "775e9c8f-aa49-5c77-bf40-dadfb16663be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254726} {:answer/id #uuid "83e30582-b220-5945-90a6-dc420162278d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254842} {:answer/id #uuid "df43e300-d37a-5ddf-bd7d-9b8787a369ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48193e72-9f23-5387-b843-0b954ae77279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254280} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cc13c9-29f6-55db-9718-7dfe7fa0c982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256348}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "558ef869-beed-50a3-ac33-63c76838d181" :question/math-academy-id "q-121407" :question/problem "The point  $(-3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,⟹\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} {:answer/id #uuid "5679b0cb-b917-5834-9cfc-21236c05bf34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254431} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddf4522-8bdc-5052-93ac-81ce5d766651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254921} {:answer/id #uuid "d96d1372-c430-5db2-a838-5534030331cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255597} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8c348e-dc48-52a8-a7a2-dcc717c081b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f927d8-7469-5f46-bcd8-ceb6220bf583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254097} :answer-field/id #uuid "65ce4959-7406-5c8f-b9dc-e08ef35e4173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256112}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e35c2dec-10c0-57fb-accb-533118e28579" :question/math-academy-id "q-121414" :question/problem "The point  $(4,8)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(4,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+3:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8)=(4,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b36618b-e713-53a3-ac40-6a8f5599cd4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254899} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1c168-5fba-5920-9504-8801e7ea3407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255124} {:answer/id #uuid "b929824d-a081-5202-b41a-f097c3bbd269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255313} {:answer/id #uuid "b939c68f-9f1f-5b41-b64e-a890960575a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255316} {:answer/id #uuid "ddeface4-f07f-5f2a-bf40-5f85d82a4184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1c168-5fba-5920-9504-8801e7ea3407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255124} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3896048-4abe-53e6-9858-4e169d6c5730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256246}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517a1517-e6eb-55b1-8c36-85bba8616819" :question/math-academy-id "q-121418" :question/problem "The point  $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3\\cdot 3)=(2,9).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,9)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(9))=(2,-9).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-9)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-9+5)=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(2)=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=9.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+5 \\\\  & =-(9)+5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036b3467-551b-5d3e-99f8-f34df741c278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290655} {:answer/id #uuid "1789665b-9485-59bf-ac4a-24f4ca8e7c6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290731} {:answer/id #uuid "4d22bf21-6fd1-521b-8cf6-ef9303eb6596" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290960} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7a2478-3275-509d-a6c9-6e581e21989f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291237} {:answer/id #uuid "d66724d1-9c6b-5173-a28b-c720d8d3a432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036b3467-551b-5d3e-99f8-f34df741c278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290655} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dcd5d3-0f90-560e-bed2-53e688d6b92b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291856}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "145a9fb2-d278-526f-9014-c06bdf23b5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-26408" :question/problem "The point $(-1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292673 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5bb3ff-2bf2-59a6-a9dc-028e36b29e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290821} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0dffee-d291-5706-b099-bf26056b0a79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291085} {:answer/id #uuid "745f11b3-4abb-5778-b95c-05bfed92c9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291130} {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} {:answer/id #uuid "f811fc7c-86fc-528d-a6a8-cba9ceee139f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46bb93d-f096-5e20-be4c-e4f183d28feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee92c98-a45f-55db-8092-7db7c00e5a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26596" :question/problem "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e45f316-de9f-5078-a264-4bec8ddfa33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291105} {:answer/id #uuid "81083a07-3ca9-51a3-aef1-cacbb9dc674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291178} {:answer/id #uuid "818351c2-9cf1-5db6-910a-2037182a0665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291179} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f07a2a-5cfb-5180-a1fd-c1ba356692a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291540} {:answer/id #uuid "f8be8001-d31c-55ee-acbf-2ca45e8e2140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81083a07-3ca9-51a3-aef1-cacbb9dc674e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291178} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fe658c-44a8-558f-a2d7-7429144d854f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292500}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d646706a-b186-5587-8de9-3f31bf5fbf11" :question/math-academy-id "q-26726" :question/problem "The point  $(4,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:  \n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2-2)=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(0))=(4,0).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,0)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,0+4)=(4,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(4)+2=2\\,⟹\\,f(4)=0.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+4 \\\\  & =-0+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059436 :question/id #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13413" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y = f(2x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,6-1) = (1,5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0737c6dd-2968-5cc9-a8f8-c0079a8268a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253724} {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} {:answer/id #uuid "75bbd111-a6df-57f9-ac2f-45af1364cecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254712} {:answer/id #uuid "8398ba0c-a692-519d-9e74-4243e928998b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254841} {:answer/id #uuid "acc2456f-4e14-5655-aa35-0e7518c63750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2173f031-3969-54c9-823a-49ff47d49386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253967} :answer-field/id #uuid "7037b1e7-eb89-5813-ae66-13b1ef312f28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256125}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bce89a1c-1788-5140-a53a-cfabf6eebe67" :question/math-academy-id "q-121221" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} {:answer/id #uuid "874eb524-3ed8-5726-b354-81594d6ba038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286028} {:answer/id #uuid "8da77226-b79f-5aa1-9fbb-eff1ddcce302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286083} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a3fa5-07d3-5f60-9264-81029b284219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286813} {:answer/id #uuid "e77beded-c213-529c-9af4-9172511b2a92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} :answer-field/id #uuid "e490dbf9-e5ff-5dfa-a559-074583566e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287616}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c0bb866-f5d6-5c26-9148-409419fa5279" :question/math-academy-id "q-186866" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233626 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "626e7d97-1fd4-5fea-b684-7fc6a67422d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-211287" :question/problem "![](images/28/282e65324ea1e7893da25ead1cc822c7c166df880c3aecc60face9adbbb7279a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $3$ units to the left, and then\n\n- translating it by $2$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x+3)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,6).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+3).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-3,6)=(-5,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units downward and get the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-5,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-5,6-2)=(-5,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2$ is $(-5,4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233627 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e205c358-cf42-5b44-9f76-2e404dd4a719" :question/math-academy-id "q-211304" :question/problem "![](images/73/73da11bf77a8bc10c00f5636176321ed1b44bbf614c8d4658e574e8ae5f85831.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=f(3x)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(15,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(3x)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(15,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(15,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 15,-5)=(5,-5).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(3x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(5,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(5,-5+4)=(5,-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=f(3x)+4$ is $(5,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233628 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a903e74e-e530-53d5-8ad7-3ef2b34613b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-211305" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-1),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-1).$\n\n- Then, we  stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot (-2),-1)=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result  along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $3$ and get the graph of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2}).$ After this transformation, the point $(-4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-4,3\\cdot (-1))=(-4,-3).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=3f(\\frac{x}{2})$ is $(-4,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233629 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e70b1b4-1844-5d99-88a9-0a513b5d410f" :question/math-academy-id "q-211461" :question/problem "![](images/57/57f2d73dd902d3e0bafec168cfe6ecd46dfc7d7a9e05f3cf33d710ca3f6b1e76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=2f(x)+5$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $5$ units up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-5),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=2f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-5).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $2$ and get the graph of $y=2f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-5)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,2\\cdot (-5))=(4,-10).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result parallel to the $y$-axis by $5$ units up and get the graph of $y=2f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-10)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-10+5)=(4,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=2f(x)+5$ is $(4,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288329} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb982b3-78f3-5bb2-88d8-08e1c9cc7f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288576} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b214ed-c977-5da8-9b98-583c8599dbc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289314} {:answer/id #uuid "cc01c367-7b62-52ca-b3f4-660a5da6d436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289386} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cb2914-db63-5a0a-b325-34eb1a5b726a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288329} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3166ba-184a-5116-9d35-4f34e933136f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290176}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e2903fa-7395-523d-a538-0dfcbb4ba792" :question/math-academy-id "q-211288" :question/problem "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f3c3352-f7de-5a7e-9ebf-a00ce3258c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288233} {:answer/id #uuid "36e46a0f-bc76-591c-9024-26e2124e639d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288401} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9c091e-f909-5d4d-b33c-8f35d5362696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288994} {:answer/id #uuid "c05a121d-0bcc-57a1-b95d-487aa2df06cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289304} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d7b031-0b65-5919-b36d-dfa7ed53d491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f3c3352-f7de-5a7e-9ebf-a00ce3258c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288233} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e9b6f7-13d8-5a6b-800b-ea453f3a0704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bba06ed1-401d-5f06-b7c3-1e90cd37f0df" :question/math-academy-id "q-211303" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff2956aa2885a0abddd614a11e632d3dfd1114391a9d7e27d790746c159e7f79.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis,\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-3f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3\\cdot (-1))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-1\\cdot (-3))=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3-1)=(4,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1$ is $(4,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b3ee48-738d-5154-93f4-593da455f6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288038} {:answer/id #uuid "02202242-35fc-558e-b9c0-5e30f23f7048" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288042} {:answer/id #uuid "7017f50c-dcb4-516e-bbbc-55369bab2f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810} {:answer/id #uuid "c4904f95-4b3c-5515-9fcf-0a99b245182b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289330} {:answer/id #uuid "cbce72d8-a3b3-5305-909c-46fd579eba75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7017f50c-dcb4-516e-bbbc-55369bab2f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288810} :answer-field/id #uuid "09418823-c229-5178-abcd-3466929f3782" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0430c7a-d01d-554d-86e5-a8ca9cccf773" :question/math-academy-id "q-211462" :question/problem "![](images/80/8000655c1943c60282d8844a2ae965b6415e21962c44309cd68806f04744b806.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis,\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $3$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **maximum** of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(2x)+3,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2,3)=(1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 3)=(1,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $3$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(2x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-3+3)=(1,0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=-f(2x)+3$ is $(1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22806c5a-b11e-585d-8a0f-a07b37da8cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288252} {:answer/id #uuid "45804e6e-0d5a-5c5c-8ca9-ac69387a3a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288504} {:answer/id #uuid "4c804f40-e7dd-5a93-ba9e-361b241596a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288561} {:answer/id #uuid "a09c844e-4df9-5435-b6c7-7f385f6c6b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289103} {:answer/id #uuid "e59fe59e-da66-54f1-9fec-c264288861a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c804f40-e7dd-5a93-ba9e-361b241596a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288561} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bbb8ab9-7f8a-598e-bca8-05a19db1385b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289859}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "064518a2-d16c-5c0a-a491-772f5bec85b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-211465" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of \n$y=-f(x-1)-2?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the right, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $2$ units down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **minimum** of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-1)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+1,3)=(3,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1\\cdot 3)=(3,-3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units down and get the graph of $y=-f(x-1)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-3-2)=(3,-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=-f(x-1)-2$ is $(3,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a28ee08-5e5f-527a-adc3-f3890854472b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288432} {:answer/id #uuid "838017c7-1d24-5ee3-ac34-a0db795b7d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288916} {:answer/id #uuid "9089b6ef-eeb8-5031-bd43-fa7c41df89e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289000} {:answer/id #uuid "99e0e358-63cd-5258-bfe8-04da80c3c6ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289059} {:answer/id #uuid "c982cb7f-975b-5883-b586-8a2935b20c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9089b6ef-eeb8-5031-bd43-fa7c41df89e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289000} :answer-field/id #uuid "9547c087-63db-52ee-8b78-af1f430f6c34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b60ced4-eb21-5887-aa63-3d04549eb22d" :question/math-academy-id "q-211466" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2d046da9a89fe09f09dbd5e0658422ab8ccee8db680a2955ebe1ff0f361b5c35.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of \n$y=-4f(3x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-4f(3x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-4f(3x).$ So, we need to find the image of $(3,1),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-4f(3x),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(3,1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,4\\cdot 1)=(1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 4)=(1,-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-4f(3x)$ is $(1,-4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2413608e-a3a6-590f-9e1e-897ac8dc08c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288261} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3a9c3e-29ce-572f-b752-3c912b43b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288330} {:answer/id #uuid "e22ce0bf-657a-50a0-8cb3-437c8db17017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289539} {:answer/id #uuid "ef97605c-5e46-5c1a-a15a-28db7c637985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289614} {:answer/id #uuid "fedf6ff2-ece5-5b19-8e86-c049a3043220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e22ce0bf-657a-50a0-8cb3-437c8db17017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289539} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c728d2-d1e5-5451-a8fc-2171c08fe3ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "633e6d77-b78e-5137-95dd-2fdc7f61a086" :question/math-academy-id "q-211467" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8d23cf36bc9fb526c0e6d041b72e6b378638f662b67aae63531c66b4a8cd6c5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the left, then\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $4$ units up.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ So, we need to find the image of $(1,-4),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-f(x+2)+4,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(1,-4).$\n\n- Then, we translate the graph by $2$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1-2,-4)=(-1,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-1\\cdot (-4))=(-1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $4$ units up and get the graph of $y=-f(x+2)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,4+4)=(-1,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-f(x+2)+4$ is $(-1,8).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059523 :question/id #uuid "1d2717f9-b905-59eb-85d7-6db4f9c9dc7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4292" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=12\\mathbf{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= (-4i + 6j) - 2(12i) \\\\\n&=-4i + 6j - 24i \\\\\n&= (-4i - 24i) + 6j \\\\\n&=-28i + 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d63a46dd-b963-5ecc-a96d-e0204ca480a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8091513a-8f2f-5ec9-9cf7-c2d508bd8c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10i + 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058975} {:answer/id #uuid "87ad0660-67f4-53a4-9f17-5dc2909a9981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4i - 8j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058976} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd76fd5-06e8-5e9a-968c-ffff7b934e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i - 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058977} {:answer/id #uuid "4af2f7e3-95fd-592d-b14e-b329daf8d287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10i - 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058978} {:answer/id #uuid "7ffb53e9-fff6-5a11-a761-7671492d0bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4i + 4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058979}] :answer-field/id #uuid "65a33916-dac4-5621-9a49-f57851b3ad93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058974}] :question/id #uuid "2d0f8d02-fc5f-57a9-b1d0-9905d55d3bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-70401" :question/problem "Find $a + b$ if $a = 7i - 6j$ and $b = 3i + 2j$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819bbd77-81fe-5b0c-9709-5c20913178d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i - 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058981} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c53f60-402e-51b4-9ae0-6ebe0157d93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058982} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f501e7-842b-5916-8760-d05182b0f466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14i + 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058983} {:answer/id #uuid "05b0b519-c4c6-5a48-bc7f-81176270be84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058984} {:answer/id #uuid "2e1600f0-3fc1-5dad-bc64-a8c5fa92fbb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14i - 2j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058985}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6e77c6a2-22c0-59a3-b06d-8d99ffe45d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058980}] :question/id #uuid "0e61282c-5ba8-52b2-b8ef-94b1e6e60711" :question/math-academy-id "q-70402" :question/problem "Find $2a + b$ if $a = 4i + 2j$ and $b = 6i - 4j$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233364 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a558acd-0b05-5aa8-b518-45323798923a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125086" :question/problem "Find $a+3b$ if $a=8i-5j$ and $b=-3i+2j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $a+3b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+3b & =(8i-5j)+3(-3i+2j) \\\\  & =8i-5j-9i+6j \\\\  & =(8i-9i)+(-5j+6j) \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233365 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d15383e5-4c36-54f6-9a69-8b61467023e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125088" :question/problem "Find $3a-2b$ if $a=-2i-2j$ and $b=4i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $3a-2b,$ we just need to compute all of the $i$'s and $j$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-2b & =3(-2i-2j)-2(4i-3j) \\\\  & =-6i-6j-8i+6j \\\\  & =(-6i-8i)+(-6j+6j) \\\\  & =-14i+0j \\\\  & =-14i\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Component Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :question/id #uuid "2cb63c87-320a-5cb4-b413-8c6bf9723641" :question/math-academy-id "e-4293" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -7, 15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 14, -11 \\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - 3 \\cdot ⟨14,-11⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - ⟨42,-33⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7 - 42, 15 - (-33)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 49, 48⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1735c445-9c97-5aa7-83d6-7dee23993153" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a296d905-1c52-5d9f-b51e-5bf51035ecb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056509} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7f554b-907a-5453-a719-30b2d5927f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨21, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056510} {:answer/id #uuid "deb9cb96-aa10-543b-8e06-8e68021d3bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨21, 7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056511} {:answer/id #uuid "5aafc1a4-0f99-5fba-91be-0462a2cfbd51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 3, 7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056512} {:answer/id #uuid "62c580e7-f5e8-5469-bbe8-7cb30c672690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3, 7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056513}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3fcc4fd2-1467-564e-9c21-a9a49176b8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056508}] :question/id #uuid "b1d8d19e-dfcf-542f-877a-8cdb7c9e0749" :question/math-academy-id "q-1304" :question/problem "If $u = ⟨12, 5⟩$ and $v = ⟨9,-2⟩$, then $u - v =$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "850889de-d865-5b67-96be-c9b833da9e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057067} {:answer/id #uuid "906c3aaf-22f6-5d7b-b468-1a3ad4cc2721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057068} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d75486-0d66-5903-8060-45073bd6b08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057069} {:answer/id #uuid "dd72efed-24e4-5a8b-9958-341bba0e1a14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨10,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057070} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8fd2a8-a0a8-54fe-a6d4-64a568ef332b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨6, 1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057071}] :answer-field/id #uuid "11ad0b88-4b59-5eec-9938-8b265f992d56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057066}] :question/id #uuid "3bcfc60f-fea9-599c-b09e-a8730963c0f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-17611" :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨ - 2, 1⟩$ and $b = ⟨4,-1⟩$. Find $a + 2b$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233383 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab7db4c4-9361-5a49-9950-d14e7b984f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-1301" :question/problem "What is the sum of $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨10,5⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{B}=⟨8,-2⟩?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨10,5⟩+⟨8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233442 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "875b5e0a-1731-539f-8cf3-8903a8a62d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-1389" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$, then $-4\\overset{\\to }{A}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-4\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-4\\cdot ⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-4)\\cdot 4,(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-16,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Using Angle Bracket Notation"}]}
